From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] GS CPU backend: bilinear texel addresses by row and column, fog and clip checks only when needed

What it is for:
  Per-pixel work of the software rasteriser, which limits Protector of
  Earth Recomp's frame rate in busy scenes.

The changes:
  - GSMem::ReadTextureQuad: the four texels of a bilinear sample with the
    page address of each row (v0, v1) and column (u0, u1) worked out once
    instead of once per texel; the same reads, in the same order, through
    the texture page buffer. SampleTextureWith uses it.
  - DrawTriangle interpolates the fog value only when PRIM.FGE is set
    (WritePixelWith reads it only then).
  - WritePixelWith takes knownInside: DrawTriangle and DrawSprite already
    clamp their loops to the scissor rectangle and skip rows of other
    bands, so they pass true (unless the scissor is inverted) and the
    per-pixel scissor and band checks are left out.
  Same results bit for bit.

How it was checked:
  ben10_gs_replay --sync: the same hashes as before on the first level's
  recording (35 points and the end) and six random recordings, and on
  three random recordings with draws sampling their own buffers
  (--any-texture 1) built before and after; 6 bands give the same hashes.
  ps2xTest 454/454. Instructions for the first 60,000 records of the
  level recording: 12.27 G -> 11.29 G (findings/20 s.34).

